k his position is untenable yes  great thank you for that now look um  all of that said  does labor think that there is any case  at all for looking at the way this is  done  that do you think there was no merit in  in patterson's claim about the way that  the process went  well i think it was extraordinary for  anybody to say there is no appeals  process in the system at the moment  because of course there is as i've  explained the standards committee  reviews the evidence they take further  evidence if necessary they can ask the  standards commissioner to go back and  look again and in this case they've  considered the web evidence overwhelming  so there already is an effect in the  appeals process but we can always  strengthen the process of course since  1695 for over 300 years all we've ever  done as parliament has strengthened that  process and what the prime minister and  jacob rees-mogg and his chief whip what  they tried to do last week was weaken it  the stannis committee is even now  looking at they are already reviewed the  code of conduct for mps as is quite  right and proper as they do every  parliament that's in process we should  let that happen chris bryant my  colleague who's chair of the spanish  committee which has got lay people on it  and it's got his cross-party  representation they should be allowed to  do their job we should support them in  doing their job when they come back with  their report we should look at it very  carefully  um  if by the way uh  mr patterson he's a former cabinet  minister and all that were recommended  for peerage um what would labour do  about it  well we obviously would not be  recommending anyone like uh mr patterson  for appearance and i hope that the prime  minister c sense and rules that out we  have called it out and said he should  not be recommended for a peerage i would  be calling and my colleagues will be  calling for that to be ruled out by the  government and that should happen at the  earliest possib